<<<
We first, perhaps, put aside the error of supposing that be requires a subjective, i.e. who, & remember that let puts wife in the objective, which raises a presumption that the same case will follow, i.e. whom;
===
We first, perhaps, put aside the error of supposing that *be* requires a subjective, i.e. *who*, & remember that *let* puts *wife* in the objective, which raises a presumption that the same case will follow, i.e. *whom*;
>>>

<<<
but then it perhaps occurs to us that the part to be played by who(m) is that of complement to may (be), which ought to be in the same case as she.
===
but then it perhaps occurs to us that the part to be played by *who(m)* is that of complement to *may (be)*, which ought to be in the same case as *she*.
>>>

<<<
In this difficulty the last resource is to write the sentence in full, Let Gilbert’s wife be her who she may be; & the insertion of the omitted her having provided the first be with the objective complement that it requires,
===
In this difficulty the last resource is to write the sentence in full, *Let Gilbert's wife be her who she may be*; & the insertion of the omitted *her* having provided the first *be* with the objective complement that it requires,
>>>

<<<
we find ourselves able to write who as the subjective complement required by the second be; who is in fact the grammatical English; cf. wHOEVER.
===
we find ourselves able to write *who* as the subjective complement required by the second *be*; *who* is in fact the grammatical English; cf. ^^WHOEVER^^.
>>>

